type":"artist","urls":["https://web.archive.org/web/20221128103419/http://galcosta.com.br:80/sec_biografia.php?id=1","https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gal_Costa","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gal_Costa","https://www.imdb.com/name/nm0182159/"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/8600/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"8600","image":"https://i.discogs.com/dtNXvgBkPzjDBEbLr9Xt_QJqsRr2OHGysUDtjddSkUs/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:378/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTg2MDAt/MTQ5NzExMTQzNy00/ODE4LmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/6FMd5wXKCFzdLBXWilZv-1U63N247t9Cq_xRnfjGmnU/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTg2MDAt/MTQ5NzExMTQzNy00/ODE4LmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"Female vocal trio originating from Philadelphia. \r\n\r\nTheir first big breakthrough was \"Armed and Extremely Dangerous\". [a=Rochelle Fleming], [a=Joyce Jones], and Chester, PA, native [a=Annette Guest] and a fourth member named Malanie McSears were originally known as the Debonettes and performed around Philadelphia.\r\n\r\nLead singer Rochelle Fleming and Annette Guest were part of a vocal quintet when both were students at Overbrook High School in Philadelphia. Rehearsing in Guest's mother's house, the group began making the rounds of talent shows and public events. After contacting WDAS Radio DJ Georgie Woods, introduced them to Philly soul guitarist, songwriter, producer and [url=http://www.discogs.com/artist/Delfonics%2C+The]The Delfonics[/url]' manager [a=Norman Harris]. By the time the group went into the recording studio, the lineup was Fleming, Guest, Wardell Piper, and Malanie McSears. The group's first release was a song written by Harris and [a=Allan Felder], the pumping \"This Is the House Where Love Died,\" which was leased to New York's Sceptor Records and issued on their Wand imprint in 1972, but failed to chart nationally. Soon after Mulaney Star left the group. \r\n\r\nIn 1973, the group signed to the Philly Groove label which was distributed by Bell Records. Their next single \"Armed and Extremely Dangerous,\" was their first big hit, going to number 11 R&B on Billboard's charts in early 1973. The track was also a Top 20 U.K. hit. Around this time, [a=Wardell Piper] exited the group and was replaced by Joyce Jones. \r\n\r\nArmed and Extremely Dangerous was released in fall 1973.  The single \"Smarty Pants,\" a cautionary tale about unwanted pregnancy, reached number 25 on the R&B charts. The album included a cover of the [a=Al Green] classic \"Love and Happiness.\" The trio's next single, the pumping \"Newsy Neighbors,\" peaked at number 35 R&B in early 1974.\r\n\r\nAround 1977, Joyce Jones left the group and was replaced by [a=Ursula Herring]. Delusions, released in fall 1977 is generally regarded as the trio's best LP, it featured lyrically rich dance tunes as well as luscious ballads and gave Philadelphia native Fleming her best showcase, displaying one of the most distinctive lead vocalists in soul/dance/pop music. Fleming's vocals are also one of the most sampled, heard on countless dance records from around the world. Their next Gold Mind LP, Hold Your Horses, was released in March 1979, with the title track becoming a disco classic. That same year, Debbie Martin replaced Ursula Herring. Around 1983 the trio split. \r\n\r\nIn the 80s, the disco influenced house music genre emerged new life was breathed into disco-soul groups like First Choice. \"Love Thang\" and \"Let No Man Put Asunder\" began to surface in club DJs' dance-floor mixes, and were met with loud enthusiasm from the crowd. Possibly bolstered by countless bootlegs, Salsoul issued a 12'' single remix of \"Let No Man Put Asunder\" by DJ [a=Frankie Knuckles]. He helped popularize the record by including it in his turntable mixes during his stints at Chicago night club The Warehouse and on his lunchtime \"hot mixes\" on local radio station WBMX-FM. Delusions was reissued on CD by U.K. label Charly in December 1994. \r\n\r\nIn 1987, Fleming reformed a version of First Choice with her cousin Laconya Fleming and Lawrence Cottel, who recorded a single for Prelude Records, \"Love Itch.\" In the '90s, Rochelle Fleming continued to record in the U.S. and Europe.\r\n","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:8600","relations":{"aliases":null,"groups":null,"members":[{"active":true,"id":28186,"name":"Rochelle Fleming"},{"active":true,"id":122141,"name":"Wardell Piper"},{"active":false,"id":211845,"name":"Joyce Jones"},{"active":true,"id":404592,"name":"Ursula Herring"},{"active":true,"id":407013,"name":"Annette Hardeman"},{"active":true,"id":563659,"name":"Debbie Martin"},{"active":true,"id":3376627,"name":"Mulaney Star"}],"namevariations":["Firss Choice","First Chiose","First Choise","First Ghoice","First Love","The First Choice","ファースト・チョイス"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/8600-First-Choice","type":"artist","urls":["http://myspace.com/firstchoicero","http://www.soulwalking.co.uk/First%20Choice.html","http://www.whosampled.com/sampled/First%20Choice/","http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Choice_(band)","https://www.imdb.com/name/nm2184121/"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/9864/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"9864","image":"https://i.discogs.com/H96u2xQG5SL_WUvyPjJFaU559wL2HNK6A2CtRmrAnz8/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:516/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTk4NjQt/MTUwMTQzMTIyMi02/MDAxLmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/wCpTlRzbbiSo7qWVRKZsCdOxtXAGWdPe8qrovatN_sk/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTk4NjQt/MTUwMTQzMTIyMi02/MDAxLmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"American singer [a67932] started to sing in church at the age of 5 years old with her family's singing group The Little David Family. At the age of 19 she started her recording career with the New Wave Soul band [a=Pal (2)], before she moved to the UK in 1991 and began her career in [a=Soul Family Sensation]. Since her solo debut in 1992 she has become an accomplished solo artist singing, writing & producing under her first name, Jhelisa. Her best known track was [url=http://www.discogs.com/Jhelisa-Friendly-Pressure/master/121047]Friendly Pressure[/url], a tune that was remixed and re-released many times. She's also been a guest singer for [a=Björk] (all backing vocals on the [url=http://www.discogs.com/master/34486]Debut[/url] album), [a=Paul Weller], [a7297], [a=Block 16], and [a=A.P.E.]. Also making guest appearances with jazz boho's like [a=Courtney Pine] and [a=Bob Belden]. Her sister is [a=Pamela Anderson], who sang with [a=D*Note] and [a=Incognito]. Her cousin is [a=Carleen Anderson], and her aunt and step-uncle are [a=Vicki Anderson] & [a=Bobby Byrd].","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:9864","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":34756,"name":"Sinden Flowers"},{"id":67932,"name":"Jhelisa Anderson"},{"id":933256,"name":"The Beat Ho"}],"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":["Jahlisa","Jelisha","Jhelisha"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/9864-Jhelisa","type":"artist","urls":["https://jhelisamusic.bandcamp.com/","https://www.facebook.com/jhelisa.anderson","https://www.imdb.com/name/nm0422576/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jhelisa_Anderson"]}},"/api/discogs/label/1175316/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"1175316","image":"","imageThumb":"","profile":"","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:1175316","relations":{"parentLabel":null,"sublabels":null},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/label/1175316-UniMotown","type":"label","urls":[]}},"/api/discogs/label/1277275/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"1277275","image":"https://i.discogs.com/MvdVG8cZwT5qkuLPoxERDlR9jjGXpFbnh-EZqXsKzZ4/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTEyNzcy/NzUtMTYwNjkyOTQ1/Ni00MjgxLmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/5m0_uip-dfDkU9niK0-cFzJzebeK1jzE8NOZm1QuR04/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTEyNzcy/NzUtMTYwNj